<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>

<!--
	The shipped read policy for the perceivable binaries this module stores (issue #227), and for
	the research runs it keeps (issue #764), which carry their report as a binary.

	A screenshot is a picture of a page somebody drove a browser to while logged in somewhere, and a
	cookie pool is that login itself. Both are reachable through "viewDocument", which loads a
	"tl.core:Binary" attribute into a model's context, so "who may read one" is not a question this
	module may leave to whoever deploys it.

	THE POLICY: the owner, plus administrators. A binary whose owner cannot be resolved - one an
	unattended run captured, with no Person to name - is readable by administrators and by code
	running in a system context, and by nobody else. That is the safe direction rather than an
	oversight: an orphan with undefined access is the one outcome this policy must not produce.

	HOW IT IS BUILT, in the three pieces below. None of them is a check of its own: the enforcement
	is the engine's "ModelAccessRights", which "ViewDocumentTool" asks through
	"ModelSecurity.canRead", so this file only says which roles that check is to find.

	  1. The role "AiBinaryOwner" exists. It is a technical role and means one thing: "this object
	     belongs to you". Nobody holds it globally - see 2.
	  2. Two role rules DERIVE it, per object, from the object's own "owner" reference. A rule of
	     type "reference" navigates a path of business attributes to the accounts that are to hold
	     the role; a path ending at a Person resolves to that Person's representative group
	     automatically. An object whose "owner" is empty therefore produces no role holder at all,
	     which is exactly the null-owner rule - written as the absence of a holder rather than as a
	     denial nobody could have read.
	  3. The <security-config> grants Read to that role, per type. Model access is deny-by-default:
	     a type with no grant for an operation resolves to an empty role set and every check against
	     it is false, so nothing here has to write a denial, and Write, Delete and Create stay with
	     the administrators they are already with.

	Administrators and code in a system context bypass the check above all of this
	(SecurityConfigurationService.isAllowedBypass), which is where the "plus administrators" half of
	the policy comes from; it is not granted here, because a grant would be a second place saying
	it.

	WHAT THIS DOES NOT DECIDE. A shared cookie pool (CookiePool#shared) is selectable by every user
	and every agent session - that is what sharing a login means, and CookiePoolService enforces it
	on the selection path, which never asks model security. Sharing a login is not the same as
	letting a model READ the stored session state, so the read policy stays owner-plus-administrator
	for a shared pool too.

	AN INSTALLATION THAT WANTS A WIDER RULE states one: add its own role to the grants below, or a
	role rule of its own that puts "AiBinaryOwner" on the objects a reporting role is to reach. It
	never has to narrow anything here, because nothing here grants more than ownership.

	See doc/security/agent-security-architecture.md section 4.6, tl-ai-core/doc/screenshots.md.
-->
<application xmlns:config="http://www.top-logic.com/ns/config/6.0">
	<services>
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.base.services.InitialRolesManager">
			<instance>
				<roles>
					<!--
						Described by "role.description.AiBinaryOwner" in this module's application
						bundle; InitialRolesManager copies that text onto the role when it creates
						it, so an administrator meeting the role in the role list is told what it
						is for.
					-->
					<role name="AiBinaryOwner"/>
				</roles>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			The two rules that put the role on an object, contributed additively to whatever access
			manager the application runs. No "class" is named on purpose: replacing the configured
			access manager from a library module would change far more than this policy is allowed
			to.

			"inherit" is set on both: a specialization of a screenshot is still a screenshot, and a
			policy that stopped at the shipped type would be silently absent for the application
			that specialized it.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.tool.boundsec.manager.AccessManager">
			<instance>
				<role-rules>
					<rules>
						<rule id="tl.ai.screenshots.owner"
							inherit="true"
							meta-element="tl.ai.screenshots:Screenshot"
							role="AiBinaryOwner"
							type="reference"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.screenshots:Screenshot#owner"/>
							</path>
						</rule>

						<!--
							A research run (Issue #764) is its owner's like a screenshot, and so are its
							parts: a source or a finding gets the role through the run that holds it,
							one rule per composition, so that the owner reads the run's tables too.
						-->
						<rule id="tl.ai.research.owner"
							inherit="true"
							meta-element="tl.ai.execution:ResearchRun"
							role="AiBinaryOwner"
							type="reference"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.execution:ResearchRun#owner"/>
							</path>
						</rule>
						<rule id="tl.ai.research.cited.owner"
							inherit="true"
							meta-element="tl.ai.execution:ResearchSource"
							role="AiBinaryOwner"
							type="reference"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.execution:ResearchRun#citedSources" inverse="true"/>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.execution:ResearchRun#owner"/>
							</path>
						</rule>
						<rule id="tl.ai.research.alsoread.owner"
							inherit="true"
							meta-element="tl.ai.execution:ResearchSource"
							role="AiBinaryOwner"
							type="reference"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.execution:ResearchRun#alsoRead" inverse="true"/>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.execution:ResearchRun#owner"/>
							</path>
						</rule>
						<rule id="tl.ai.research.findings.owner"
							inherit="true"
							meta-element="tl.ai.execution:ResearchFinding"
							role="AiBinaryOwner"
							type="reference"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.execution:ResearchRun#findings" inverse="true"/>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.execution:ResearchRun#owner"/>
							</path>
						</rule>
						<rule id="tl.ai.research.otherfindings.owner"
							inherit="true"
							meta-element="tl.ai.execution:ResearchFinding"
							role="AiBinaryOwner"
							type="reference"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.execution:ResearchRun#otherFindings" inverse="true"/>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.execution:ResearchRun#owner"/>
							</path>
						</rule>

						<rule id="tl.ai.cookiepool.owner"
							inherit="true"
							meta-element="tl.ai.cookiepool:CookiePool"
							role="AiBinaryOwner"
							type="reference"
						>
							<path>
								<step attribute="tl.ai.cookiepool:CookiePool#owner"/>
							</path>
						</rule>
					</rules>
				</role-rules>
			</instance>
		</config>

		<!--
			Read, and only Read. What an owner may do with their own screenshot beyond looking at it
			- delete it, hand it to somebody - is a question this ticket did not answer, and a grant
			written on a guess is harder to take back than one that was never there.
		-->
		<config service-class="com.top_logic.model.security.SecurityConfigurationService">
			<instance>
				<security-config>
					<class name="tl.ai.screenshots:Screenshot">
						<grant
							inherit="true"
							operation="Read"
							roles="AiBinaryOwner"
						/>
					</class>
					<class name="tl.ai.execution:ResearchRun">
						<grant
							inherit="true"
							operation="Read"
							roles="AiBinaryOwner"
						/>
					</class>
					<class name="tl.ai.execution:ResearchSource">
						<grant
							inherit="true"
							operation="Read"
							roles="AiBinaryOwner"
						/>
					</class>
					<class name="tl.ai.execution:ResearchFinding">
						<grant
							inherit="true"
							operation="Read"
							roles="AiBinaryOwner"
						/>
					</class>
					<class name="tl.ai.cookiepool:CookiePool">
						<grant
							inherit="true"
							operation="Read"
							roles="AiBinaryOwner"
						/>
					</class>
				</security-config>
			</instance>
		</config>
	</services>
</application>
